e year. \n\nI realize these figures don't represent the total outstanding, but only the amounts maturing in specific years. The table's complexity makes it challenging to extract the full picture of securities outstanding. I need to dig deeper into the raw data to understand the complete total of fixed maturity securities.\n\nThe data suggests the initial extraction might be correct, focusing on the primary \"Total\" values for each year. I'll carefully examine the context to confirm this interpretation.\n\nThe table reveals a nuanced pattern of total fixed maturity securities across different years, with 1948 showing a significant value of 46,615. This appears to capture the total fixed maturity securities at the start of each year, providing a consistent time series for analysis.\n\nMy initial observations align with this interpretation, indicating the extracted data represents meaningful total values for each year. The OLS regression will help project these trends forward.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_e87d886b977e4c77b2d7044a","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Find and extract data on interest-bearing public marketable securities (fixed maturity) from Treasury Bulletin documents for Jan 1948-1951\n- [x] Fit OLS linear regression model to project value for end of January 1952\n- [x] Round result to nearest tenths place\n- [ ] Write final answer to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
