tal (46,615), for Jan 31, 1949 we take 36,068, etc.\n\nThis interpretation seems reasonable given the data structure and my analysis. So the answer 39.5 billion should be correct.\n\nHowever, I'm now thinking about whether the values should be interpreted differently. \n\nThe data consistently shows the total fixed maturity securities at each date, with the first \"Total\" row capturing the complete outstanding amount. The pattern remains clear: 46.615 billion in 1948, decreasing to 36.068 billion in 1949, then rising to 44.467 billion in 1950, and finally dropping to 40.537 billion in 1951. This trajectory supports the regression model prediction of 39.5 billion for 1952.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_a44d3ca5aeb040babf098554","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Find and extract data on interest-bearing public marketable securities (fixed maturity) from Treasury Bulletin documents for Jan 1948-1951\n- [x] Fit OLS linear regression model to project value for end of January 1952\n- [x] Round result to nearest tenths place\n- [x] Write final answer to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
